# auto-network-location - network identification rules
#
# Copy to ~/.wifi-loc-control/locations.env and edit. One numbered group per
# network. LOCATION_n_NAME is the macOS network location to switch to, and the
# address/MAC pair identifies the network.
#
# The MAC must belong to a device that is present while the Mac is on that
# network, whether or not it is the Mac's current gateway; an upstream router
# is a good choice. Collect each pair while connected to that network:
#
#     ./wifi-loc-detect.sh --print-mac <device address>
#
# A group may also name an optional TARGET device: the one this location's own
# settings depend on (its gateway or DNS). It answers a second question -- "is
# this still the network my settings were written for?" -- and defaults to the
# feature device above, so most networks need nothing extra.
#
# This file is sourced by the script, so it is code: keep it owned by you,
# mode 600, and do not copy one in from an untrusted source.

# Home network, identified by its upstream router
LOCATION_1_NAME="Home"
LOCATION_1_IP="192.0.2.1"
LOCATION_1_MAC="00:00:5e:00:53:01"

# Optional. If you give one, give it in full: a malformed target skips the
# whole group rather than being silently ignored.
# LOCATION_1_TARGET_IP="192.0.2.100"
# LOCATION_1_TARGET_MAC="00:00:5e:00:53:02"
# Another network, identified by its own router
# LOCATION_2_NAME="Office"
# LOCATION_2_IP="198.51.100.1"
# LOCATION_2_MAC="aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff"
